## Why This Is Asked

Tests error handling discipline, prevents data leakage, and demonstrates practical logging and traceability in a real stack.

## Key Concepts

- Centralized error handling with a dedicated AppError class
- Distinguish log content from client response to avoid leakage
- Environment-based verbosity (prod vs dev)
- RequestId for cross-service tracing
- Consistent HTTP status mapping for common errors

## Code Example

```javascript
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message)
    this.statusCode = statusCode
    this.isOperational = true
  }
}

function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  const code = err.statusCode || 500
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'
  if (isProd) {
    // Do not leak details
    res.status(code).json({ error: 'Something went wrong', code })
  } else {
    // Development: expose details for debugging
    res.status(code).json({ message: err.message, stack: err.stack, code })
  }
  // Log full details securely (stack, req data, requestId) in both modes
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test error handling across prod/dev? 
- How can you propagate requestId across microservices for end-to-end tracing?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical, beginner-friendly encryption practices that map to real-world apps without backend changes.

## Key Concepts
- Encryption at rest with AES-GCM
- Password-based key derivation (Argon2id)
- OS-level key storage (Keychain/Keystore)
- Key rotation and re-encryption on login

## Code Example
```javascript
// Derive key from password and encrypt a note (simplified)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function deriveKey(password, salt) {
  // Argon2id in real code; use placeholder PBKDF2 for illustration
  return crypto.pbkdf2Sync(password, salt, 100000, 32, 'sha256');
}
function encryptNote(key, plaintext) {
  const iv = crypto.randomBytes(12);
  const cipher = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv);
  const enc = Buffer.concat([cipher.update(plaintext, 'utf8'), cipher.final()]);
  const tag = cipher.getAuthTag();
  return { iv: iv.toString('hex'), data: enc.toString('hex'), tag: tag.toString('hex') };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate keys with minimal downtime?
- What telemetry would you collect to detect misuses of the keychain?

